Our intent in this case - Define a family of algorithms, encapsulate each one and make them interchangeable.
Assume we have a few variants to a specific algorithm like sorting,
we have bobble sort, quicksort, etc... eventually they all do the same thing with different advantages.
So the solution will be to define a strategy API (abstract class or interface) let each algorithm variant implement this API

and instead of many conditionals, move branches into their own Strategy class.
Use this pattern to avoid exposing complex, algorithm specific details.
Then we create a factory and the client decides the variation upon creation.

we will use the sorting problem as Example:
public interface SortStrategy {
    void sort(Comparable[] data);
}

public class QuickSort implements SortStrategy{
    public void sort(Comparable[] data) {...}
}

public class MergeSort implements SortStrategy{
    public void sort(Comparable[] data) {...}
}

public class SortStrategyFactory{
    public static SortStrategy select(...) {...}
}

public class SomeCollection {
    private Comparable[] contents;
    private SortStrategy sorter;

    public SomeCollection() {
        this.sorter = SortStrategyFactory.select(...);
        }
    public void sortContents() {
        this.sorter.sort(this.contents);
        ....
        }
}
